Answer 1 (Shmuel Goldstein): To better understand the core meaning of the word כַּפֶּר, it would be beneficial to compare and contrast it with similar Hebrew words. As part of a familiar phrase found in the Machzor of Yom Kippur, we have three such words all grouped together. This is the phrase of סְלַח לָֽנוּ - Pardon us, מְחַל לָֽנוּ - Forgive us, כַּפֶּר לָֽנוּ - Absolve us. As is the case with Hebrew words, especially those found within Torah, each of these has its own distinct meaning. Since the word מְחַל doesn’t appear once in TaNaKh in the context of forgiveness, we’ll first focus on the words סְלַח and כַּפֶּר. Rabbeinu Bachya writes, “There is a difference between what is described as כַּפָּרָה“atonement,” and סְלִיחָה, “forgiveness.” The word סְלִיחָה means that the guilt has been forgiven לְגַמְרֵי - totally, is now “non-existent” whereas the former is merely concerned with culpability for the sin committed.” [Rabbeinu Bachya ben Asher- Vayikra 5:10.] Also, the notes in the Artscroll Machzor of Yom Kippur explain, “According to Malbim [See also Vayikra 4:26 where the Malbim explains that כַּפָּרָה is what the sinner needs to do and סְלִיחָה is what we ask of HaShem.] and R' Hirsch, these three terms go from a greater degree of forgiveness to a smaller degree. סְלַח is the idea of total forgiveness; מְחַל means a permanent deferral of punishment, even though the sin has not been completely forgiven; כַּפֶּר - from כפרה, cover - implies that the sin is hidden from sight so that the punishment is suspended temporarily.” [Artscroll’s Yom Kippur Machzor, page 860. C.f. Shenei Luchos HaBris, Aseres HaDibros, Yoma, Ner Mitzva 1, where he states that these three words indicate an increasing level of forgiveness corresponding to decreasing levels of sinfulness.] Elsewhere, Rav Sampson Rafael Hirsch explains this root word כַּפֶּר on its first appearance when describing the instructions for the building of the Ark for Noach. It says, “וְכָפַרְתָּ אֹתָהּ מִבַּיִת וּמִחוּץ בַּכֹּפֶר… - and you must cover it both inside and outside with pitch.” HaRav Hirsch explains, [The Hirsch Chumash, Bereishis 6:14, pg. 182.] “The basic meaning of כפר (related to: גבר - strength, קבר - bury) is a protective or restrictive covering. It protects something so that nothing from the outside affects it. So is the case here, and the same is it elsewhere on the word כַּפֹּרֶת.” [Shemos 25:17.] As mentioned above, the word מְחַל doesn’t appear once in TaNaKh in the context of forgiveness but also has the meaning of a type of covering, a cave, which offers protection from harm. In Yeshayahu it says, “וּבִמְחִלּוֹת עָפָר - and into the hollows of the earth…” where Rashi explains: מחלות: הם הם מערות, i.e., caves. [Yeshayahu 2:19.] Avraham’s nephew, Lot, also sought out protection in a cave while Sodom and its sister cities were being destroyed.[Midrash HaGadol, Bereishis 19:30.] Dovid HaMelech also sought out refuge in the same cave of Adullam[Sefer HaYashar ad.loc.] with his family while escaping from King Shaul. [I Samuel 21:11-16, 22:1.] So it can be suggested that the phrase “סְלַח לָֽנוּ - Pardon us, מְחַל לָֽנוּ - Forgive us, כַּפֶּר לָֽנוּ - Absolve us” can be understood as follows; “סְלַח לָֽנוּ - Pardon us completely. And if not, at least מְחַל לָֽנוּ - Forgive us, and protect us as if in a cave so we may not suffer punishment. And if not, at least כַּפֶּר לָֽנוּ - Forgive us – cover and shield us at least temporarily to avoid punishment.[ C.f. Ibn Ezra on אֲכַפְּרָה פָנָיו, Bereishis 32:21.] {From the upcoming sefer Divrei Shaagasi }

Answer 2 (Dov): In the back of the Artscroll Machzor on Yom Kippur (p.860) on the part of vidui where we read: ועל כֻּלָּם אֱלֽוֹהַּ סְלִיחוֹת. סְלַח לָֽנוּ. מְחַל לָֽנוּ. כַּפֶּר לָֽנוּ And for all of these, G-d of pardon, pardon us, forgive us, grant us atonement. It writes as follows: This is a progression from a smaller to a greater degree of forgiveness. סלח, forgive us , i.e. give up the right to punish us, מחל, pardon us , i.e. do not even harbour resentment or ill will against us; and כפר, atone for us , i.e. remove any effects of our sin as if we had never committed them. ( Abarbanel ) According to Malbim and R' Hirsch , these terms go from a greater degree of forgiveness to a smaller degree. סלח connotes total forgiveness; מחל means a permanent waiver of the right to punish, even though the sin has not been completely forgiven; כפר - from כפרה, cove r - implies only that the sin is hidden from sight, so that the punishment is suspended temporarily. In Pathway to Prayer by Rabbi Mayer Birnbaum he adopts the first approach, and translates it as: סְלַח לָֽנוּ - Forgive us for the sins themselves מְחַל לָֽנוּ - Pardon us for the insult caused to Your Honor by our sins כַּפֶּר לָֽנוּ - (and) atone for us as if they had never occurred.

Answer 3 (shmosel): The Shaloh explains that they're increasing level of forgiveness corresponding to decreasing levels of sinfulness: כפרה לחטאים. ומחילה לעונות. וסליחה לפשעים שחטא הוא השוגג מתפללים שימחקם לגמרי כאלו לא היו מעולם. וזה ל' כפרה כמו אכפרה פניו וגו'. ומחילה לעון כי שם נשאר שום רושם. וסליחה שהיא אריכות אף לפושעים הם המורדים כי עליהם יום הכפורים תולה For חטא, which is an inadvertent sin, we pray that He should erase them completely, like they never happened. This is [indicated by] the term כפרה, similar to "I will appease him." (Genesis 32:21) [The weaker term] מחילה corresponds to עון, because it leaves some trace behind. סליחה connotes clemency to פושעים, the wanton sinners, because Yom Kippur only suspends [judgement] for them.
